EVIL BOT — BOT SERVER PATCH
============================

Your uploaded bot already has the real pairing call:

  sock.requestPairingCode(pairPhone, 'JAMESDEV')

and an existing HTTP health server in index.js.

Do NOT expose your panel password/token to the website.

1) Add this environment variable on the BOT SERVER:

PAIR_API_SECRET=make-a-long-random-secret-here

2) Replace the existing health-server callback inside startPingLoop()
   (the http.createServer((req,res)=>{ ... }) block) with the version below.

IMPORTANT:
- Keep your existing startPingLoop() function.
- This uses Node's built-in http module, so no Express package is required.
- The website sends POST /api/pair with JSON: {"phone":"234..."}.
- The endpoint waits for the REAL Baileys pairing code and returns it.

CODE:

  http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
    const origin = process.env.WEBSITE_URL || '*';

    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', origin);
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET, POST, OPTIONS');
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'Content-Type, x-pair-secret');

    if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') {
      res.writeHead(204);
      return res.end();
    }

    if (req.method === 'GET' && req.url === '/health') {
      const uptime = formatUptime(Date.now() - global.botStartTime);
      res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
      return res.end(JSON.stringify({
        status: 'alive',
        uptime,
        sessions: activeSockets.size
      }));
    }

    if (req.method === 'POST' && req.url === '/api/pair') {
      if (req.headers['x-pair-secret'] !== process.env.PAIR_API_SECRET) {
        res.writeHead(401, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
        return res.end(JSON.stringify({ error: 'Unauthorized' }));
      }

      let raw = '';
      req.on('data', chunk => {
        raw += chunk;
        if (raw.length > 4096) req.destroy();
      });

      req.on('end', async () => {
        try {
          const body = JSON.parse(raw || '{}');
          const pairPhone = String(body.phone || '').replace(/\D/g, '');

          if (!/^\d{7,15}$/.test(pairPhone)) {
            res.writeHead(400, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
            return res.end(JSON.stringify({ error: 'Invalid phone number' }));
          }

          const ownerTgId = settings.OWNER_TELEGRAM_ID || 'web';
          const sessionId = `web_${ownerTgId}_${pairPhone}`;

          if (activeSockets.has(sessionId)) {
            res.writeHead(409, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
            return res.end(JSON.stringify({
              error: 'A session for this number is already active.'
            }));
          }

          let finished = false;

          const finish = (status, payload) => {
            if (finished) return;
            finished = true;
            res.writeHead(status, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
            res.end(JSON.stringify(payload));
          };

          const timer = setTimeout(() => {
            finish(504, { error: 'Pairing code request timed out.' });
          }, 30000);

          try {
            await startWhatsApp(
              sessionId,
              null,
              pairPhone,
              null,
              async (code) => {
                clearTimeout(timer);
                finish(200, {
                  code,
                  uptime: formatUptime(Date.now() - global.botStartTime)
                });
              }
            );
          } catch (e) {
            clearTimeout(timer);
            finish(500, { error: e.message });
          }
        } catch (e) {
          res.writeHead(400, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
          res.end(JSON.stringify({ error: 'Invalid JSON request' }));
        }
      });

      return;
    }

    const uptime = formatUptime(Date.now() - global.botStartTime);
    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ status: 'alive', uptime, sessions: activeSockets.size }));
  }).listen(process.env.PORT || 3001, () => {
    logSuccess('PING', `Health/API server :${process.env.PORT || 3001}`);
  });

3) On Render create the website as a Web Service.

Environment variables:

BOT_API_URL=https://YOUR-BOT-DOMAIN.example.com/api/pair
BOT_API_SECRET=THE-SAME-RANDOM-SECRET

4) On your bot panel/server set:

PAIR_API_SECRET=THE-SAME-RANDOM-SECRET
WEBSITE_URL=https://YOUR-RENDER-SITE.onrender.com

5) The frontend contains NO JavaScript.
The normal HTML form POSTs to /pair. The Render Node server proxies
that request to your bot API.

6) Your existing Baileys call remains responsible for the real code.
Do not replace requestPairingCode() with a random code generator.

Security:
- Use a long random PAIR_API_SECRET.
- Do not put PAIR_API_SECRET in index.html or CSS.
- Do not put Telegram/GitHub tokens in the website.
- Consider adding rate limiting before making the pairing endpoint public.
